{
  "title": "My family — and an upside-down \"have\"",
  "title_target": "Моя сім'я — Moya simya",
  "goal": "Talk about your family, say who you have and don't have, and understand why Ukrainian doesn't say \"I have\" but \"at me there is\".",
  "bekal": [
    "name the members of a family",
    "say what you have with У мене є",
    "negate it with немає",
    "use the possessives мій, моя, моє, мої",
    "say how many people are in your family"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Діалог — A family photo on the phone screen",
      "intro": "Andriy looks at a photo on Ani's phone. Notice that there isn't a single verb meaning \"have\" in this conversation — only \"at me there is\" and \"at me there isn't\".",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Це|Tseh] [твоя|tvo-YA] [сім'я|seem-YA]?",
          "id": "Is this your family?",
          "literal": "this — your (f) — family?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Так|Tak]. [Це|Tseh] [моя|mo-YA] [мама|MA-ma], [а|a] [це|tseh] [мій|meey] [тато|TA-to].",
          "id": "Yes. This is my mom, and this is my dad.",
          "literal": "yes. this — my (f) — mom, and — this — my (m) — dad."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[А|A] [хто|khto] [це|tseh]?",
          "id": "And who's this?",
          "literal": "and — who — this?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Це|Tseh] [моя|mo-YA] [сестра|ses-TRA]. [Її|Yee-YEE] [звати|ZVA-ti] [Діна|DEE-na].",
          "id": "This is my younger sister. Her name is Dina.",
          "literal": "this — my — sister. her (object) — they-call — Dina."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[У|OO] [тебе|TEH-beh] [є|yeh] [брат|brat]?",
          "id": "Do you have a brother?",
          "literal": "at — you (родовий) — there-is — brother?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Так|Tak], [у|oo] [мене|MEH-neh] [є|yeh] [брат|brat]. [Йому|Yo-MOO] [двадцять|DVAD-tsyat'] [років|RO-keev].",
          "id": "Yes, I have an older brother. He's twenty.",
          "literal": "yes, at — me — there-is — brother. to-him — twenty — years."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[А|A] [в|v] [мене|MEH-neh] [немає|neh-MA-yeh] [брата|BRA-ta]. [Тільки|TEEL'-ki] [сестра|ses-TRA].",
          "id": "I don't have a brother. Just a sister.",
          "literal": "and — at — me — there-isn't — brother (родовий). only — sister."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Твоя|Tvo-YA] [сестра|ses-TRA] [тут|toot], [у|oo] [Києві|KI-yeh-vee]?",
          "id": "Is your sister here, in Kyiv?",
          "literal": "your — sister — here, in — Kyiv?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Ні|Nee], [вона|vo-NA] [у|oo] [Львові|L'VO-vee], [з|z] [батьками|bat'-KA-mi].",
          "id": "No, she's in Lviv, with our parents.",
          "literal": "no, she — in — Lviv, with — parents."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[У|OO] [тебе|TEH-beh] [велика|veh-LI-ka] [сім'я|seem-YA]?",
          "id": "Is your family big?",
          "literal": "at — you — big (f) — family?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Не|Neh] [дуже|DOO-zheh]. [Нас|Nas] [четверо|CHET-veh-ro].",
          "id": "Not very. There are four of us.",
          "literal": "not — very. us (родовий) — a-foursome."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[А|A] [нас|nas] [шестеро|SHES-teh-ro]. [У|OO] [нас|nas] [ще|shcheh] [є|yeh] [кіт|keet].",
          "id": "There are six of us. We also have a cat.",
          "literal": "and — us — a-sixsome. at — us — also — there-is — cat."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Ukrainian has no everyday verb for \"to have\".** It flips the sentence: not \"I have a brother\" but **У мене є брат** — \"at me there is a brother\". The owner goes into the родовий after the preposition *у*; the thing owned becomes the subject of the sentence.",
        "**The verb *мати* does exist, but it's rare in conversation.** *Я маю брата* is grammatically correct and common in formal writing, but at the corner shop, at home, and on the street people say *У мене є*. Use the form you'll actually hear.",
        "**У and в are the same word here.** *У мене* and *в мене* are both correct and mean the same. It's just about what's easier to say: after a vowel people tend to use **в**, after a consonant **у**. Andriy does exactly that in line seven.",
        "**To negate having, use немає — and the thing changes its ending.** *У мене є брат* → *У мене **немає брата***. This is the second doorway into the родовий: absence is **always** followed by the родовий, with no exceptions at all. Notice also that **є** disappears — *немає* already contains it.",
        "**Немає is one word, written solid.** It's a merger of *не* + *має*, but it fused long ago. ⚠️ Writing *не має* as two words means something else (\"doesn't have\", with a clear subject) and is rarely needed by beginners.",
        "**Possessives follow the gender of the thing owned, not the owner.** *Мій тато* (masculine) and *моя мама* (feminine) — both said by Ani, a woman. What decides the ending is the noun that follows. That's the opposite of English *his* / *her*, and it's where beginners slip most often.",
        "**Counting people uses a special form.** Not *ми чотири* but **нас четверо** — literally \"of us a foursome\". These forms (збірні числівники, collective numerals) are only used for people: *нас двоє, троє, четверо, п'ятеро, шестеро*."
      ],
      "grammars": [
        {
          "title": "У мене є — possession turned upside down",
          "body": [
            "Change the pronoun; the preposition stays. This is one of the few tables in this course that's really worth memorizing whole, because you'll use it every day."
          ],
          "table": {
            "head": [
              "English",
              "Ukrainian",
              "Say it",
              "Literally"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "I have",
                "у мене є",
                "oo MEH-neh yeh",
                "at me there-is"
              ],
              [
                "you have (informal)",
                "у тебе є",
                "oo TEH-beh yeh",
                "at you there-is"
              ],
              [
                "he has",
                "у нього є",
                "oo NYO-ho yeh",
                "at him there-is"
              ],
              [
                "she has",
                "у неї є",
                "oo NEH-yee yeh",
                "at her there-is"
              ],
              [
                "we have",
                "у нас є",
                "oo nas yeh",
                "at us there-is"
              ],
              [
                "you have (formal / plural)",
                "у вас є",
                "oo vas yeh",
                "at you there-is"
              ],
              [
                "they have",
                "у них є",
                "oo nikh yeh",
                "at them there-is"
              ]
            ]
          },
          "after": [
            "To ask a question, the word order doesn't change at all — just raise your voice at the end: **У тебе є брат?**"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Немає + родовий: absence",
          "body": [
            "Compare in pairs. Notice that the noun changes its ending the moment it's absent."
          ],
          "table": {
            "head": [
              "There is",
              "There isn't",
              "English"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "У мене є брат.",
                "У мене немає брата.",
                "have / don't have a brother"
              ],
              [
                "У мене є сестра.",
                "У мене немає сестри.",
                "have / don't have a sister"
              ],
              [
                "У нас є кіт.",
                "У нас немає кота.",
                "have / don't have a cat"
              ],
              [
                "У них є машина.",
                "У них немає машини.",
                "have / don't have a car"
              ],
              [
                "У мене є час.",
                "У мене немає часу.",
                "have / don't have time"
              ],
              [
                "Тут є кафе.",
                "Тут немає кафе.",
                "there's a / there's no café here"
              ]
            ]
          },
          "after": [
            "**Немає** is also used on its own for \"there's no…\": *Проблем немає* (no problem), *Часу немає* (there's no time). Two sentences you'll hear a lot."
          ]
        },
        {
